{"notes":"THE CORE AND THE HOOK LANDED 2026-11-24. crates/mimid-agent/src/acme.rs: the account persisted to acme-account.json, the order for the [tls] names, the DNS-01 challenge, the certificate kept beside local-ca's, renewal at two thirds of an assumed 90-day lifetime through the reload loop; [tls.acme] with directory, contact, dns, hook and root; dns = "hook" runs the operator's command with the record's name and value and takes a zero exit as proof it is live. tests/acme.rs drives a whole order against a fake ACME authority (HTTP/1.1 over the test certificate, ALPN pinned so the client does not pick h2), so it stays offline.

THIS BEAD STAYS OPEN, because its title promises three DNS methods and one is built. The other two are now children: mimid-e13m.8.1 (RFC 2136 with TSIG) and mimid-e13m.8.2 (acme-dns). Of the two, .8.2 is about 100 lines through the write_record seam that already exists and is worth doing; .8.1 is a DNS wire-format implementation of 400 to 600 lines that an operator can get today with a three-line hook calling nsupdate, so weigh it before starting.

FOUND WHILE BUILDING IT: the HTTPS client inside instant-acme builds its configuration from rustls's PROCESS-WIDE default CryptoProvider and panics when there is none. Everything else in mimid names its provider explicitly (decision mimid-1wgh.26), so source = "acme" would have panicked on first use in a real daemon. acme::ensure now installs aws-lc-rs once, and only if nothing else has, so an embedder's own choice survives."}
